# guardz (runtime type guards)

**Package:** `guardz` · **Doc:** [docs/conventions/guardz-and-structure-validation.md](../../docs/conventions/guardz-and-structure-validation.md)

## When to use

- Parsing `unknown` from HTTP bodies, WebSocket messages, SSE, `JSON.parse`, or third-party APIs
- Validating nested DTOs before business logic runs
- New `is*.ts` guards in features or `src/lib/`

## Required patterns

- Import guards from **`guardz`** (`isString`, `isNonEmptyString`, `isType`, `isOneOf`, `isArrayWithEachItem`, `isUndefinedOr`, `isNonNullObject`, …)
- Prefer **`isType<Interface>({ … })`** over manual property checks when the shape has multiple fields
- Keep **types** in `*.type.ts`; keep guards in separate `is*.ts` or `*.guardz.ts` files (see `rules-typescript-validation-and-typeguards.mdc`)
- Use **`readonly`** on DTO interfaces; use **`Nullable<T>`** from guardz for nullable fields when appropriate

## Shared re-exports (daily-magic)

- `@/lib/agentWitch/isRecord` → `isNonNullObject` from guardz
- `@/lib/agentWitch/isNonEmptyString` → `isNonEmptyString` from guardz

Prefer guardz directly in new code when there is no legacy import path.

## Forbidden

- New hand-rolled `typeof x === "object" && x !== null` helpers when `isNonNullObject` / `isRecord` applies
- Duplicating guardz under `src/utils/typeguards/` (EnergyCenter pattern — not this repo)
- Using `any` to skip validation at boundaries

